cturing or eliminating\" doesn't choose between rung 3 (keep current protections, market rents for new building), rung 4 (rent rules only on subsidized units) and rung 5 (no rent control at all).\n- **Two weaknesses in that passage:**\n  - It is dated before his campaign, so I coded it `pre-seating` (V5).\n  - Calling it his own words is borderline. Hilton signed the foreword, but the report names Edward Ring as lead author of the body, and the rent-control text is in the body. I coded it `own-words` / `statement-other` and recorded the doubt in the passage note.\n- **The rest:** the starter-home page, the VMT page and the CNN debate housing answer are all about building costs and supply, with nothing on rent rules. I coded them `adjacent`. The other three sources I left out, because none of them mention rent or tenants.\n- **`needs_source`:** Hilton's own campaign-era words on rent control or AB 1482, the 2019 statewide rent cap, such as a candidate questionnaire or a debate answer.\n- **Quote:** the report sentence above, tiered `direction`. It fails the accountability test (V7 T2) because it doesn't commit to restructuring or to eliminating.","ttft_ms":14863,"type":"result","duration_ms":37533,"uuid":"3f02ed25-c794-4e75-a85d-ff32fe30a37e","ttft_stream_ms":3113,"time_to_request_ms":329,"first_content_frame_ms":3113,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
